{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994455,"nonce":"ftJoxDQrKtMZXVn9zszovpxWwTM=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUrl9g","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/209","origin":"github"},"title":"lu(backend=\"pardiso\") calls a 3-D Taylor-Hood Stokes system singular when the form carries a parameter","message":"`jno.solve.lu(backend=\"pardiso\")` refuses a 3-D Taylor-Hood Stokes system as singular as soon as the form carries a parameter. The same system without a parameter solves with PARDISO, and `backend=\"host\"` solves both. jNO's check is loud, which is right; the system is not singular.\n\nReproduced on release 0.4.2 and on `main` (b3dcdd22).\n\n## What differs\n\nPrinting the matrix type handed to `PyPardisoSolver`:\n\n| Problem | Parameter in the form | PARDISO `mtype` | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3-D, 11,732 unknowns | no | 11 (real, non-symmetric) | solves |\n| 3-D, 11,732 unknowns | per-cell viscosity | -2 (real, symmetric indefinite) | \"factorized a SINGULAR operator ... perturbed 15 pivot(s) ... relative residual 5.32e+00\" |\n| 2-D, 4,722 unknowns | per-cell viscosity | -2 | solves |\n\nSo the parametric path delivers the saddle-point system in a form that jNO classifies as symmetric indefinite, and PARDISO's symmetric indefinite factorisation (pivot perturbation, no pivoting across supernodes) then fails on the 3-D system. The same happens compiled and not compiled.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n`python stokes.py pardiso param` raises, `python stokes.py pardiso plain` and `python stokes.py host param` solve.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nimport jax\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport jno\n\ninner, grad, trace = jno.np.inner, jno.np.grad, jno.np.trace\nbackend, mode = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]  # host | pardiso, plain | param\nL, H, E = 1.2, 0.4, 1e-9\n\nd = (jno.shape.box(0, 0, 0, L, H, H, size=0.16) - jno.shape.sphere(0.35, 0.2, 0.2, 0.1, size=0.05)).domain()\nd.tag(\"inlet\", lambda x, y, z: x \u003c E)\nd.tag(\"noslip\", lambda x, y, z: (y \u003c E) | (y \u003e H - E) | (z \u003c E) | (z \u003e H - E) | ((x - 0.35) ** 2 + (y - 0.2) ** 2 + (z - 0.2) ** 2 \u003c 0.101**2))\n_, s_nu = d.fem_symbols(space=\"P0\", names=(\"r_nu\", \"s_nu\"))\nnu = jno.np.parameter(s_nu, name=\"nu\") if mode == \"param\" else 1.0\ncells = max(len(c) for c in d.mesh.cells_dict.values())\nu, v = d.fem_symbols(value_shape=(3,), names=(\"u\", \"v\"), order=2)\np, q = d.fem_symbols(names=(\"p\", \"q\"), order=1)\nX = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)[:3]\ninlet, noslip = (d.variable(r, split=True)[:3] for r in (\"inlet\", \"noslip\"))\nat = lambda f, Y: f.bind(**dict(zip(\"xyz\", Y)))\ngu, gv = grad(u, X), grad(v, X)\nprofile = 16.0 * inlet[1] * (H - inlet[1]) * inlet[2] * (H - inlet[2]) / H**4\nfem = jno.fem([\n    nu * inner(gu, gv, n_contract=2) - at(p, X) * trace(gv),\n    -at(q, X) * trace(gu),\n    u(*inlet)[0] - profile,\n    u(*inlet)[1] - 0.0,\n    u(*inlet)[2] - 0.0,\n    u(*noslip) - (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n])\nlu = jno.solve.lu(backend=backend)\nsol = fem.solve(linear=lu, nu=jnp.ones(cells)) if mode == \"param\" else fem.solve(linear=lu)\nprint(backend, mode, \"unknowns\", fem.dofs, \"max |u| =\", float(jnp.max(jnp.abs(sol))))\n```\n\n```\npardiso plain unknowns 11732 max |u| = 179.3972214425198\nhost param unknowns 11732 max |u| = 179.39722144251743\npardiso param: RuntimeError: MKL PARDISO factorized a SINGULAR operator (11732x11732): it perturbed 15 pivot(s)\nand the solution has relative residual 5.32e+00. ...\n```\n\n## Possible fixes (none tested)\n\n- For a system with a zero diagonal block, hand PARDISO the general type (`mtype` 11), as the parameter-free path does.\n- Or keep `mtype` -2 and switch on scaling and weighted matching (`iparm(11) = 1`, `iparm(13) = 1`), which MKL recommends for highly indefinite symmetric matrices such as saddle-point systems. They are off by default for this type.\n\nRelated: #190 (PARDISO perturbs tiny pivots on a cover-space operator with void; scaling fixes it there).","files":null}]}